About Me (the User)
Who am I in the context of this problem? What roles am I playing (student, employee, parent, athlete, gamer…)?
I am a multi-console gamer playing long, systems-heavy campaigns across PS5, PC and Switch  during evening and weekend sessions, often taking breaks between plays.

The last 3 times this happened: Real, recent moments. For each: when and where, what went wrong, what it cost, what you did.
I had already picked up a missable upgrade core before draining the Flooded Cathedral, spent 35 minutes backtracking through empty rooms, and hit a severe frame-pacing hitch at a sluice valve without knowing a 10-second camera workaround existed.

Resumed from Quick Resume into pitch-black ground textures, spent 40 minutes searching Discord and Reddit threads to figure out whether my save file was corrupted or if it was a known bug,and quit early out of frustration.

Wanted to verify whether frame drops in a boss arena were caused by my display preset or a recent patch, recorded a gameplay clip.

What it costs me: Be specific. How often, how much?
⏱️ Time: 2–3 hours per week spent backtracking through cleared zones, skimming long wiki pages, and hunting through scattered threads for console-specific fixes.

💸 Money: Occasional wasted expansion purchases or second-copy buys when unresolved performance regressions make one console version feel broken.

🔋 Energy: Context switching fatigue from juggling 5 to 8 browser tabs, Discord searches and google searches during a short evening play window.

🙂 Joy: Boss encounters are spoiled by wiki headers and broken immersion when an unexpected hitch or crash derails a session.

2. Problem Statement
Gut check: I need one calm, spoiler safe companion for my campaign progress, console specific bug workarounds, and gameplay performance clips because splitting walkthrough notes, hardware glitches, and frame-rate benchmarks across wikis, chat servers, and video folders wastes my play sessions.


I need [X] because [Y].
Five Whys:
Why do I lose momentum mid-campaign?
Why do I get stuck on unexpected crashes or stutters?
Why can't I tell if a frame-rate dip is my hardware's fault or a patch regression?
Why do wikis and social feeds fail to connect these pieces?
Why does that hurt my gaming overall?

Problem statement: 
I sometimes run into a sudden glitch or frame drop, I struggle to remember where I left off without seeing spoilers, locate a verified fix for my exact console, and pair gameplay footage with frame-rate numbers, which costs me 2 to 3 hours of play time each week.This happens because of the console specific bug workarounds, and performance clips live in disconnected places with no shared structure. Right now I juggle browser wiki tabs, google searches, but those sources spoil upcoming bosses, bury working fixes under old patches, and video proof from YouTube.

I'll know this is solved when:
I can sit down, see my next uncompleted chapter without seeing a boss spoiler and check whether an issue on my console already has a confirmed workaround.

Check: none of these words appear: app, website, platform, dashboard, tool, AI, track, automate, notify, feature.
Existing Solutions


What I use or tried
What it does well
Why it falls short for me
Game Wikis (IGN / Fextralife)
Detailed item descriptions, maps, and full walkthrough prose
Static pages with no personal checklist persistence, heavy ad clutter, and frequent late-game spoilers.
Reddit & Discord Servers
Fast community reactions right after a patch drops
PS5, Xbox, PC, and Switch issues are mixed together; verified workarounds get buried within hours.
Console Capture Gallery + Notes
Saves raw video clips and quick personal text reminders
Isolated from chapter progress, community workarounds, and structured FPS / 1% low comparisons.
Technical Breakdown Videos (YouTube)
Deep launch-week frame-rate analysis across consoles
Read-only; you cannot upload your own zone captures or filter metrics by current patch and zone.




The gap: There is no single, structured workspace that connects personal spoiler safe chapter progress, console filtered bug workarounds, and playable user gameplay clips with frame rate telemetry.

